vo_avfoundation: stamp a media-time frame after every frame before it

On media time (iOS and iPadOS, picture in picture's clock) each frame's
sample is stamped with its timestamp. VOCTRL_RESET's flush drops the
samples the display layer still holds, but not the ones CoreMedia already
took for display, up to queue-lead-ms ahead of where the old file was; a
seek's timebase jump never showed them, but the next file's timestamps
start over. Skip from an episode a second in, and a second into the next
one the frame of the skipped episode at that timestamp flashed in place of
the new one, or stayed up if the pause landed on it.

Media stamps now never go back either (avf_media_stamp_offset): a frame
whose stamp would be at or before the last one moves an offset added to
every later timestamp, so it is stamped just after the old frames and they
show before it, as the old file's last frames, never after. A redraw keeps
the stamp of the frame it shows again. The layers' clock runs on the stamps:
picture in picture's time range is the clock less the position, which the
offset does not change. Host mode (tvOS, macOS hosts that ask for it) keeps
its own rule.

A libmpv program on macOS 27.2 that loads A, B (played 1 s) and C with
loadfile replace, holds each first frame 100 ms as a player's start gate
does, and reads the frame the display layer shows every 2 ms
(copyDisplayedPixelBuffer): with --avfoundation-presentation=media, a frame
of B showed in C's playback in 3 of 8 runs with VideoToolbox and 1 of 8 in
software, each at its own timestamp's slot; host mode 0 of 8. The same on
the iPhone 17 simulator under Plezy: a frame of the skipped episode at
1.208 s and at 3.833 s, between the next one's frames. scripts/
test_avf_media_stamp.sh checks the rule.

